\DOC LOCAL_MONTSQR_P256_TAC

\TYPE {LOCAL_MONTSQR_P256_TAC : int -> string list -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Advances an {eventually}-style machine-code goal past an inlined
Montgomery-squaring subroutine call, replacing the current symbolic state by a
successor state and adding assumptions describing the subroutine's effect.

\DESCRIBE
The first argument is an {int} selecting a variant/offset for the
instruction-counting of the call site; the observed traces use 0 and 1, which
shift the successor state index. The second argument is a {string list} of two
names: the first names the variable holding the squared output written to
memory, the second names the variable whose square is computed. The goal's
conclusion is an {eventually} assertion about a state variable, and the tactic
rewrites it to the same assertion about a fresh successor state variable,
yielding exactly one subgoal. Assumptions mentioning the old state —
program-counter value, {bytes_loaded}, register contents, memory-block
contents, and the accumulated {MAYCHANGE} relation — are discharged and
re-added with the new state variable, with the program counter updated to the
instruction after the call and the {MAYCHANGE} relation extended with the
memory block written by the subroutine; the memory-contents assumption for the
destination block now equates it to the named output variable. A new assumption
is added stating that, under a bound hypothesis on the squared input, the named
output equals the Montgomery product of the input with itself modulo the field
prime. Assumptions not referring to the state (nonoverlapping facts and earlier
arithmetic characterisations) are kept, though their order may change.

\COMMENTS
All observed traces show this single kind of state-advancing transformation on
goals of this machine-code form.

\ENDDOC
